<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1833 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">God Save the Tsar!</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ru">Боже, Царя храни!</bdi> / <bdi lang="ru-Latn">Bozhe, Tsarya khrani!</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The anthem of the Russian Empire from 1833 until the revolution of 1917, a short prayer for the tsar.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Vasily Zhukovsky</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Alexei Lvov</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>The Russian Empire</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Russian Empire</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>chosen in a competition in 1833; first performed 18 December 1833</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Vasily Zhukovsky, music by Alexei Lvov (1833))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-tsar">God Save the Tsar!</a>&quot; was the national anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. It was chosen in a competition held in 1833 and first performed on 18 December 1833. The music was composed by the violinist Alexei Lvov and the words were written by the court poet Vasily Zhukovsky.</p><p>It remained the anthem until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917. The new government then adopted the &quot;Worker's Marseillaise&quot;, which served until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-provisional-government">Russian Provisional Government</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> later that year. In the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> the composition was used as the royal anthem, imposed by the temporary Russian government and the commanders of the Russian army; Bulgaria gave it up after its declaration of independence in 1908.</p><p>Many composers used the theme. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Tchaikovsky</a> quoted it in the 1812 Overture, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> Slave, his overture on the Danish national anthem and the Festival Coronation March. In the Soviet era the authorities altered Tchaikovsky's scores, putting other patriotic melodies, such as the &quot;Glory&quot; chorus from Glinka's opera A Life for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a>, in its place. Charles Gounod wrote a Fantasy on the Russian National Hymn on it.</p><p>The tune also travelled far from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 1842 the English writer Henry Chorley set his hymn &quot;God, the Omnipotent!&quot; to Lvov's melody, and as the &quot;Russian Hymn&quot; it was printed in 19th- and 20th-century hymnals and still appears in English-language hymnals of several American churches. American colleges, schools and fraternities set their own songs to it. Film and television used it as a sign of imperial Russia: Maurice Jarre's score for Doctor Zhivago (1965), William Walton's for Three Sisters (1970) and the BBC's War and Peace (1972). The Royal Scots Dragoon Guards still play it at formal events in tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>, who had been colonel-in-chief of their predecessor regiment, the Scots Greys. In 1998 the rock singer Alexander Gradsky proposed that the theme become Russia's national anthem again, with words quite different from Zhukovsky's.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A short prayer that God protect the tsar, written for the court as the empire's anthem. The mood is solemn and hymn-like, which is why the tune passed so easily into church hymnals.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Russian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Russian</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Боже, Царя храни!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Bože, Carja xrani!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God, save the Tsar!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Сильный, державный,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Silʹnyj, deržavnyj,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Strong, sovereign,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Царствуй на славу нам,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Carstvuj na slavu nam,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>reign for our glory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Царствуй на страх врагам,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Carstvuj na strax vragam,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>reign for the dread of [our] enemies,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Царь православный;</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Carʹ pravoslavnyj;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Orthodox Tsar;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Боже, Царя храни!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Bože, Carja xrani!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God, save the Tsar!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Vasily Zhukovsky (1833). Text from <a href="https://ru.wikisource.org/wiki/%D0%93%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%BD_%D0%A0%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (scientific transliteration).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: scientific transliteration (х = x, ц = c, ж = ž, й = j, ы = y, я = ja, ь = ʹ). This text is printed in the modern spelling (no ъ, і or ѣ), so there is nothing of the old orthography to transliterate.</li><li>line 2: державный (deržavnyj) is 'sovereign, ruling', from держава, 'realm' and also the orb of royal power.</li><li>lines 3-4: на славу нам / на страх врагам: literally 'to glory for us' / 'to fear for enemies', i.e. reign so that we have glory and the enemies dread; 'our' before enemies is supplied.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">1812 Overture (Tchaikovsky)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Marche Slave (Tchaikovsky)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Festival Coronation March (Tchaikovsky)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Overture on the Danish national anthem (Tchaikovsky)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fantaisie sur l'Hymne National Russe (Gounod)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">God, the Omnipotent! (Henry Chorley)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1842</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words (the &quot;Russian Hymn&quot; of English hymnals)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hail, Pennsylvania!</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (alma mater of the University of Pennsylvania)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Firm Bound in Brotherhood</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (song of the Order of the Arrow)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Doctor Zhivago (Maurice Jarre's score)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1965</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Three Sisters (William Walton's score)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1970</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">War and Peace (BBC)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1972</span><span class="sg-chain-h">theme music</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec"><h2>People, places and events</h2><div class="tih-tags"><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Alexei Lvov</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Vasily Zhukovsky</span><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Mikhail Glinka</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Charles Gounod</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">William Walton</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Henry Chorley</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Maurice Jarre</span><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Alexander Gradsky</span><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Principality of Bulgaria</span><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Bulgarian Declaration of Independence</span></div></section>
